  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nOther than a gradual warm-up through the end of the week, \nlittle day-to-day change in the weather is expected from now \nthrough Friday. This is due to the center of an area of high \npressure sliding east across the International Border the rest \nof today through midday Thursday, keeping clear skies in place \nalong with light winds overnight (which may promote patchy to \nareas of fog early Thursday morning) followed by winds swapping \nto a SE direction which will promote slow warm air advection \nacross the region. This will allow for high temperatures today,\nThursday and Friday running some 15-20 degrees above normal,\nincluding the potential for highs hitting 80 degrees over\nsouthwest MN Thursday and Friday. \n\nThe pattern change starts late Friday into Saturday as a well-\norganized area of low pressure develops over the Northern\nRockies Thursday night into Friday, then rapidly shifts NNE into\ncentral Canada over the weekend. A cold front sagging south from\nthis system will sweep across the Upper Midwest Saturday through\nSunday. The system will lose a lot of its Pacific-origin\nmoisture as it moves across the Rockies and into Canada, but\nenough frontal lift combined with its remaining moisture may be\nenough to squeeze out some scattered rain showers Saturday\nafternoon into late Sunday. Although the front will be well SE \nof the WFO MPX coverage area come Sunday into early next week, a\nfew weak upper level shortwaves may be able to interact with \nthe stalled front to produce a few showers from time to time \nacross our area for the first have of the next work week.\n\nThe more noticeable change will be with respect to temperatures\nbehind the cold front. We may have hit the point of the last 70+\ndegree high temperatures as strong cold air advection will knock\nback high temperatures into more seasonable levels of the low-\nmid 50s. Similarly, lows will drop into the mid 30s to mid 40s\nfor at least the start of next week. While CPC outlooks still\nhighlight above normal temperatures through the rest of this\nmonth, normals fall to the upper 40s to lower 50s by the end of\nthis month. Thus, with those lower normals and continuing\nlowering sun angle, \"above\" normal highs will likely remain \nonly in the 60s and have a very hard time returning to the 70s.",
